WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:01.644
Here’s the whole idea.

2
00:00:01.644 --> 00:00:06.699
Most UI tests fail because a selector moved, not because the product is broken.

3
00:00:06.699 --> 00:00:12.114
Intent QA stores what the user is trying to do, and what must remain true.

4
00:00:12.114 --> 00:00:19.335
It can recover a harmless interaction change, but it will not rewrite an assertion or explain away a real bug.

5
00:00:19.335 --> 00:00:25.112
Let me show you the entire developer experience, from a clean checkout to the correctness boundaries.

6
00:00:25.767 --> 00:00:28.587
I’m starting from a clean clone.

7
00:00:28.587 --> 00:00:35.166
Installation is ordinary npm: install the dependencies, initialize the QA workspace, and validate it.

8
00:00:35.166 --> 00:00:40.806
Init is idempotent, so it creates anything missing without overwriting an edit.

9
00:00:40.806 --> 00:00:47.855
Validation applies the schemas and the cross-file checks before we spend time opening a browser.

10
00:00:47.855 --> 00:00:51.615
A spec list confirms the repository is ready.

11
00:00:52.267 --> 00:00:55.851
Now I create a test from a normal requirement.

12
00:00:55.851 --> 00:01:02.620
I choose the local environment, reuse the login fixture, and state the visible outcome I care about.

13
00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:04.611
The result is editable YAML.

14
00:01:04.611 --> 00:01:09.787
Notice what is not here: no CSS selector, XPath, coordinate, or fixed sleep.

15
00:01:09.787 --> 00:01:18.547
The intent and expectation are the source of truth, and the CLI and workspace UI validate and save the exact same file.

16
00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:25.446
For execution, I ask the autonomous QA skill to run checkout card on local.

17
00:01:25.446 --> 00:01:32.138
This is the important boundary: Codex drives the real app through its native Browser capability.

18
00:01:32.138 --> 00:01:41.507
The reusable fixture signs in, the agent opens the cart, reaches checkout, submits the approved test payment, and observes the confirmation.

19
00:01:41.507 --> 00:01:45.522
The workspace picks up the completed file-backed result automatically.

20
00:01:45.522 --> 00:01:51.768
Every step passed, the accessible targets are recorded, and the screenshots explain the decision.

21
00:01:52.433 --> 00:01:56.137
Now I change the application, not the test.

22
00:01:56.137 --> 00:02:01.694
Proceed to checkout moves into a menu and becomes Continue to payment.

23
00:02:01.694 --> 00:02:05.862
I rerun the same spec with every expectation unchanged.

24
00:02:05.862 --> 00:02:17.438
The old target is gone, so the agent captures that failure, rediscovers one explicitly equivalent accessible control, retries once, and checks the original checkout-form expectation.

25
00:02:17.438 --> 00:02:18.364
It passes.

26
00:02:18.364 --> 00:02:23.921
That is a healed interaction, supported by before-and-after evidence—not an edited requirement.

27
00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:26.771
Next, a real functional bug.

28
00:02:26.771 --> 00:02:33.284
The checkout interaction still works, but the application says the order could not be completed.

29
00:02:33.284 --> 00:02:38.928
There is nothing to heal: the action succeeded and the required outcome failed.

30
00:02:38.928 --> 00:02:47.177
The result stays a functional regression, and the original assertion still says that an order confirmation must be visible.

31
00:02:47.177 --> 00:02:53.256
This is the correctness guarantee—the agent never negotiates the expectation to manufacture a pass.

32
00:02:53.933 --> 00:02:57.736
Finally, behavior succeeds but the approved design does not.

33
00:02:57.736 --> 00:03:04.498
The action appears before the heading, and the green success treatment becomes a red warning panel.

34
00:03:04.498 --> 00:03:10.415
Because the functional steps passed, the separate design checkpoint runs at its declared viewport.

35
00:03:10.415 --> 00:03:16.754
The result shows the explicit reference, the actual screenshot, and concrete order and style findings.

36
00:03:16.754 --> 00:03:23.093
That supports a design regression; it is not the agent grading the page by taste.

37
00:03:23.766 --> 00:03:25.404
Everything remains inspectable.

38
00:03:25.404 --> 00:03:34.142
Specs, results, screenshots, and the last-run pointer are ordinary repository files, so the UI is optional.

39
00:03:34.142 --> 00:03:46.156
Ninety-seven tests enforce the path guards, atomic saves, immutable expectations, healing boundary, classification rules, design evidence, demo resets, and the localhost workspace.

40
00:03:46.156 --> 00:03:57.078
That is the complete loop: install, describe intent, run the native browser, recover harmless drift, and keep real regressions visible.
